/// Text compares as text, whatever type it arrived as.
///
/// A Char widens to a String, so the two compare equal when they hold the same
/// code points.  Both processors report False instead -- silently, which is the
/// worst of the three available answers: a language may convert them, or refuse
/// the comparison, but reporting two identical pieces of text as different
/// gives the program no way to notice.
///
/// ⚠️ The collection cases are the ones that bite.  Equality is what Contains,
/// Map keys and case labels are built on, so today a Map keyed by 'A' cannot be
/// found with Str('A') and a List holding 'A' does not contain it.  A program
/// meets this as a lookup that mysteriously misses rather than as a type error.
///
/// Fails with: Assertion 'true' failed.
///
test 'A Char and a String compare as text';
begin
    var Letter : Char   := 'A';
    var Word   : String := Str ('A');

    AssertTrue  (Letter = Word);
    AssertTrue  (Copy ('ab', 0, 1) = 'a');
    AssertTrue  (not (Letter <> Word));

    var Seen := Map ();
    Seen.Put ('A', 'yes');
    AssertTrue (Seen.Contains (Str ('A')));

    AssertTrue (['A'].Contains (Str ('A')));
end
